window.docContents[96].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___7___-The-Jordan-Holder-theorem/Length-of-a-representation-and-Jordan-Holder-series/#Chapter3___Discussion_after_Theorem3___7___1":{"contents":"The Jordan-Holder theorem shows that the number n of terms in a filtration of V with irreducible successive quotients does not depend on the choice of a filtration and depends only on V. This number is called the length of V. It is easy to see that n is also the maximal length of a filtration of V in which all the inclusions are strict.\n\nThe sequence of the irreducible representations W_1, \\ldots, W_n enumerated in the order they appear from some filtration of V as successive quotients is called a Jordan-Holder series of V.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.7. The Jordan-Holder theorem\u0009Length of a representation and Jordan-Holder series","header":"Length of a representation and Jordan-Holder series","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___7___-The-Jordan-Holder-theorem/Length-of-a-representation-and-Jordan-Holder-series/#Chapter3___Discussion_after_Theorem3___7___1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/SU_LPAR_2_RPAR____-quaternion-algebra___-and-SO_LPAR_3_RPAR_/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___7":{"contents":"Problem 4.12.7. Let G = SU(2) (the group of unitary 2 \\times 2 matrices with determinant 1), and let V = \\mathbb{C}^2 be the standard 2-dimensional representation of SU(2). We regard V as a real representation, so it is 4-dimensional.\n\n(a) Show that V is irreducible (as a real representation).\n\n(b) Let \\mathbb{H} be the subspace of \\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb{R}}(V) consisting of endomorphisms of V as a real representation. Show that \\mathbb{H} is 4-dimensional and closed under multiplication. Show that every nonzero element in \\mathbb{H} is invertible, i.e., \\mathbb{H} is an algebra with division.\n\n(c) Find a basis 1, i, j, k of \\mathbb{H} such that 1 is the unit and\n\ni^2 = j^2 = k^2 = -1, \\quad ij = -ji = k, \\quad jk = -kj = i, \\quad ki = -ik = j.\n\nThus we have that Q_8 is a subgroup of the group \\mathbb{H}^{\\times} of invertible elements of \\mathbb{H} under multiplication.\n\nThe algebra \\mathbb{H} is called the quaternion algebra, and its elements are called quaternions. Quaternions were discovered by W. R. Hamilton in 1843 (see Section 4.13).\n\n(d) For q = a + bi + cj + dk, a, b, c, d \\in \\mathbb{R}, let \\bar{q} = a - bi - cj - dk and ||q||^2 = q\\bar{q} = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2. Show that \\overline{q_1 q_2} = \\bar{q}_2 \\bar{q}_1 and ||q_1 q_2|| = ||q_1|| \\cdot ||q_2||.\n(e) Let G be the group of quaternions of norm 1. Show that this group is isomorphic to SU(2). (Thus geometrically SU(2) is the 3-dimensional sphere.)\n\n(f) Consider the action of G on the space V \\subset \\mathbb{H} spanned by i, j, k, by x \\to qxq^{-1}, q \\in G, x \\in V. Since this action preserves the norm on V, we have a homomorphism h : SU(2) \\to SO(3), where SO(3) is the group of rotations of the 3-dimensional Euclidean space. Show that this homomorphism is surjective and that its kernel is \\{1, -1\\}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.12. Problems\u0009SU(2), quaternion algebra, and SO(3)","header":"SU(2), quaternion algebra, and SO(3)","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/SU_LPAR_2_RPAR____-quaternion-algebra___-and-SO_LPAR_3_RPAR_/#Chapter4___Problem4___12___7"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/Semisimplicity-of-k_LSQ_G_RSQ_-implies-characteristic-does-not-divide-___G___/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem--Semisimplicity-of-k_LSQ_G_RSQ_-implies-characteristic-does-not-divide-___G___--Semisimplicity-of-k_LSQ_G_RSQ_-implies-characteristic-does-not-divide-___G___--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"There is a complete pairwise nonisomorphic family of simple finite-dimensional representations whose endomorphism algebras form an algebra equivalent to the group algebra, and whose squared dimensions sum to the group order.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Proposition4.1.2/Derived2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Theorem4.1.1; role=primary]\n\nIf the group algebra of a finite group over a field is semisimple, then the field-valued cardinality of the group is a unit.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Discussion_before_Proposition4.1.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.1.3/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Proposition4.1.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.1. Maschke's theorem\u0009Semisimplicity of k[G] implies characteristic does not divide |G|\u0009Semisimplicity of k[G] implies characteristic does not divide |G|\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem/Semisimplicity-of-k_LSQ_G_RSQ_-implies-characteristic-does-not-divide-___G___/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___1___-Maschke___s-theorem--Semisimplicity-of-k_LSQ_G_RSQ_-implies-characteristic-does-not-divide-___G___--Semisimplicity-of-k_LSQ_G_RSQ_-implies-characteristic-does-not-divide-___G___--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Alternative-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/#Chapter4___Discussion_after_Theorem4___6___3":{"contents":"Theorems 4.6.2 and 4.6.3 imply Maschke's theorem for complex representations (Theorem 4.1.1). Thus, we have obtained a new proof of this theorem over the field of complex numbers.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations\u0009Alternative proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations","header":"Alternative proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Alternative-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/#Chapter4___Discussion_after_Theorem4___6___3"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Module-categories-as-abelian-categories/#Chapter7___Example7___7___2":{"contents":"Example 7.7.2. The category of modules over an algebra A and the category of finite dimensional modules over A are abelian categories.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.7. Abelian categories\u0009Module categories as abelian categories","header":"Module categories as abelian categories","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Module-categories-as-abelian-categories/#Chapter7___Example7___7___2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact--Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"If the displayed property holds for the source abelian category, an additive functor sends a short exact complex to a short exact complex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Example7.9.5; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Discussion_after_Example7.9.5/Derived01; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Additive functors on semisimple categories are exact\u0009Additive functors on semisimple categories are exact\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact--Additive-functors-on-semisimple-categories-are-exact--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product/#Chapter7___Example7___9___6":{"contents":"Example 7.9.6. (i) The functors \\operatorname{Ind}_K^G, \\operatorname{Res}_K^G are exact.\n\n(ii) The functor \\operatorname{Hom}(X, ?) is left exact, but not necessarily right exact. To see that it need not be right exact, it suffices to consider the exact sequence\n\n0 \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z} \\to \\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z} \\to 0\n\nand to apply the functor \\operatorname{Hom}(\\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z}, ?).\n\n(iii) The functor X \\otimes_A for a right A-module X (on the category of left A-modules) is right exact but not necessarily left exact. To see this, it suffices to tensor multiply the above exact sequence by \\mathbb{Z}/2\\mathbb{Z}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Exactness properties of Ind, Res, Hom, tensor product","header":"Exactness properties of Ind, Res, Hom, tensor product","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Exactness-properties-of-Ind___-Res___-Hom___-tensor-product/#Chapter7___Example7___9___6"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Lifting-homomorphisms-through-extensions/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Lifting-homomorphisms-through-extensions--Lifting-homomorphisms-through-extensions--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For an exact pair of linear maps and projective source modules, constructs a linear map from their direct sum with a prescribed restriction on the left summand and prescribed composite on the right summand.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Exercise8.1.4; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Lifting homomorphisms through extensions\u0009Lifting homomorphisms through extensions\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Lifting-homomorphisms-through-extensions/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Lifting-homomorphisms-through-extensions--Lifting-homomorphisms-through-extensions--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary predicate on functions from a finite decidable index type to a ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.1.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.1. Lifting of idempotents\u0009Complete system of orthogonal idempotents\u0009Complete system of orthogonal idempotents\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Complete-system-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___2___-Projective-covers--Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules--Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary statement whose displayed formal type contains an elided term.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.2.1; role=primary]\n\nEvery finite projective module satisfying the designated module property is linearly equivalent to a member of a projective family with Kronecker-delta linear-map dimensions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.2.1; role=primary]\n\nA family of finite projective modules with Kronecker-delta linear-map dimensions decomposes the regular module as a direct sum with multiplicities given by the dimensions of the simple modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Theorem9.2.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Classification of indecomposable projective modules\u0009Classification of indecomposable projective modules\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___2___-Projective-covers--Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules--Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules--Formalization--Primary-declarations"}});